# ADA-compliant color usage

- Aim for **WCAG 2.1 Level AA** contrast: **4.5:1** for normal text, **3:1** for large text, and **3:1** for non-text UI where applicable.
- **Interactive states:** Check contrast for **default**, **`:hover`**, **`:focus`** / **`focus-visible`**, and **`active`** when those states change foreground or background. Passing at rest but failing on hover or focus is not acceptable for text or essential controls.
- Do not rely on **color alone** for meaning; add text, icons, or patterns when needed.
- Prefer design tokens (e.g. `tokens.colors` in [src/lib/mit-sailing/tokens.ts](src/lib/mit-sailing/tokens.ts)) or shared theme variables instead of one-off hex values without verification.
- **Secondary-looking** text on tinted backgrounds (e.g. highlights) still needs sufficient contrast; prefer the same strong foreground token as body text (e.g. `tokens.colors.text`) and differentiate with typography, not low-contrast gray, unless contrast is verified.
- **Opacity-only hovers** (e.g. `hover:opacity-70`) lower effective contrast. Verify the **blended** result against the background, not only base hex values.
- When adding new pairs, use a contrast checker (e.g. [WebAIM Contrast Checker](https://webaim.org/resources/contrastchecker/)) and include **effective** colors after overlays in the check.

**Example:** If a link uses `color: #750014` (MIT Red web) at rest and `hover:opacity-70`, compute or sample the hover color on the real background and confirm both ratios meet AA—not only the rest state.

- **Automated regression:** `npm run test:e2e:a11y` runs `@axe-core/playwright` with WCAG 2.x A/AA rule tags on public marketing URLs (from live `/sitemap.xml` plus curated routes) in **light and dark** theme. Use it as a net for many machine-detectable issues; translucent layers, gradients, and motion still need design-token and manual checks.
